        drivingExperience: "The driving experience of the CLC 160 BlueEFFICIENCY is heavily focused on smooth, comfortable cruising. While 129 horsepower is relatively low for a heavy coupe, the Roots-type supercharger provides instant, lag-free torque (220 Nm) right off idle, making the car feel much punchier in city traffic than the numbers suggest. The chassis, which is essentially carried over from the older W203 C-Class, provides a very traditional, insulated, and heavy Mercedes-Benz ride quality. The steering is somewhat vague but highly comfortable, and the optional 5-speed automatic slurs gears together seamlessly. It is an excellent, stylish commuter car, but it completely lacks the sharp dynamic handling found in its BMW or Audi rivals.",
        mechanicVerdict: "The M271 engine in the late-model CLC 160 is a double-edged sword. On one hand, the core block is fantastic, and the supercharger provides brilliant drivability while the port-injection system prevents any carbon buildup issues. On the other hand, the single-row timing chain is a catastrophic design flaw. If you buy this car, you must treat the timing chain as a 100,000 km wear item, full stop. If you ignore it, it will stretch, snap, and destroy the engine. Furthermore, you must immediately check if the camshaft magnet isolation pigtails are installed; if not, oil may have already destroyed the ECU. If you are diligent with oil changes and proactive about the timing chain and PCV hoses, this engine is highly rewarding and incredibly durable. If you neglect maintenance, it will financially ruin you.",
