        author: "Mercedes-Benz CLC 2008-2011 (https://skfb.ly/pzzHU) by Merc_TV is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
        popupAnimationName: "",
    
        drivingExperience: "The CLC 200 Kompressor offers a noticeably more enthusiastic driving experience than the 143 hp CLC 180, thanks to its 184 hp and 250 Nm output. The Eaton supercharger continues to provide instant, lag-free throttle response, but the extra power makes the car feel genuinely brisk rather than merely adequate. The torque swell from the mid-range gives confident motorway overtaking and satisfying acceleration out of corners. The supercharger whine is slightly more audible under load, adding character to the four-cylinder soundtrack. The 5-speed automatic, when healthy, keeps the engine in its sweet spot effectively, though it lacks the rapid-fire shifts of modern dual-clutch gearboxes. The CL203 chassis is a compact, predictable handler with comfortable ride quality, though it ultimately traces back to the older W203 platform rather than a cutting-edge sports coupe. Steering is accurate but filtered, suiting the car's grand touring pretensions more than back-road aggression. When the timing chain, supercharger, and mounts are in good health, the driving experience is smooth, refined, and pleasantly quick for its era. Neglect introduces rattles, vibration, hesitation, and a growing sense that the car is fighting itself, which is particularly disappointing given the stronger powertrain's potential.",
    
        mechanicVerdict: "The M271.957 in the CL203 CLC 200 Kompressor is fundamentally the same engine as the lower-output M271.946, but with modified pistons and higher boost pressure to deliver 184 hp and 250 Nm. This makes it a more desirable variant from a driving perspective, yet it inherits every single catastrophic vulnerability of the M271 family without exception. The first and most infamous flaw is the single-row timing chain. Mercedes-Benz made a cost-driven engineering decision to use a narrow single-row chain with plastic guide rails and a hydraulic tensioner that is entirely dependent on oil pressure and viscosity. When oil changes are stretched beyond 10,000 kilometers, or when low-quality oil is used, the tensioner bleeds down overnight. The next cold start produces a telltale rattle from the front of the engine as the loose chain slaps the guides. If this rattle is ignored, the chain stretches further, the guides shatter, and the chain jumps teeth on the sprockets. Because the M271 is an interference engine, the pistons will collide with the open valves, bending them, damaging the guides, and usually requiring a complete cylinder head rebuild or replacement. The 184 hp variant places marginally greater dynamic load on the timing system during spirited driving, but the fundamental failure mode and prevention strategy remain identical: short oil intervals with correct synthetic oil, listening for cold-start rattle, and proactive chain replacement before failure. The second catastrophic flaw is the camshaft adjuster solenoid oil leak into the ECU harness. The front-mounted intake camshaft adjuster magnets have seals that harden over time. Oil seeps past these seals into the electrical connectors, and through capillary action, the oil wicks up the wiring loom and travels all the way back to the ECU mounted on the bulkhead. Once oil contaminates the ECU connector and circuit board, the engine management system begins to fail intermittently, producing misfires, rough running, and eventually complete immobilization. A replacement ECU is astronomically expensive and requires professional cloning or dealership programming. The only defense is proactive seal replacement before any oil is visible in the harness. Beyond these headline flaws, the Eaton supercharger is a robust unit that is frequently killed by simple ignorance. It has its own sealed oil cavity that requires periodic draining and refilling with specific Eaton fluid. Most owners never do this, and most general workshops are unaware it is even necessary. The bearings eventually fail, producing a loud whine or rattle, and a replacement or rebuild is far more expensive than routine oil service. The oil cooler and filter housing gasket is another common leak point that drips oil onto the exhaust heat shield, creating a persistent burning smell. The plastic thermostat housing is prone to cracking, and the crankshaft position sensor is a frequent source of sudden no-start episodes. The CL203 platform itself adds chassis vulnerabilities; the rear subframe and floor pan are highly rust-prone in certain climates, which can render an otherwise mechanically salvageable car a structural write-off. If you are considering purchasing a CLC 200 Kompressor, your inspection priorities must be: documented proof of timing chain replacement with a full kit, physical inspection of the cam adjuster harness for any trace of oil, verification that the supercharger oil has been serviced, a thorough rust inspection of the rear subframe and jacking points, and evidence of regular synthetic oil changes at short intervals. The 184 hp output is a meaningful improvement over the 143 hp variant and makes the car genuinely enjoyable to drive, but it does not transform the M271 into a more robust engine. Attempting to extract significantly more power through aggressive tuning is mechanically unwise given the known fragility of the single-row chain and the supercharger's bearing limits. Treat it as a comfortable, stylish cruiser with just enough forced-induction character to feel special, maintain it obsessively, and it will reward you. The 2008-2011 facelift CLC models are generally preferred over earlier C-Class SportCoupé versions because they addressed some interior and trim quality issues, though the core M271 mechanical package remained unchanged. In summary, this is a 7 out of 10 risk rating engine not because it is inherently weak, but because its survival is entirely conditional on educated, proactive ownership. The reward for that diligence is a genuinely pleasant 184 hp supercharged coupe with instant throttle response and classic Mercedes refinement."
        ,
    
        repairCostEstimate: "900 - 1,900 EUR / year for a well-maintained example, but catastrophic failure years can exceed 4,000 EUR. The most critical budget items are the timing chain service (600-900 EUR if done preventatively; 3,000+ EUR if it fails and destroys the head), the cam adjuster seal service (80-150 EUR preventatively vs. 1,500+ EUR for ECU replacement), and the supercharger oil service (120 EUR vs. 800+ EUR for a rebuild). Routine costs include synthetic oil changes every 10,000 km (90-110 EUR), transmission service every 60,000 km (280 EUR for automatic, 90 EUR for manual), suspension bushing replacements (200 EUR), and thermostat or cooling system repairs (150-300 EUR). Rust remediation is highly variable and can range from a simple underbody treatment to structural subframe replacement exceeding 2,000 EUR. The key financial strategy with this engine is to spend money on prevention; every euro spent on the timing chain, cam seals, and supercharger oil before failure is worth five euros saved on catastrophic repairs."
