        timingType: "Single-row timing chain mounted at the front of the engine. The M271 family is notorious for a cost-driven decision to use a narrow single-row chain rather than the more robust double-row designs found on earlier Mercedes engines. Over time, the chain elongates as the plastic guide rails degrade, the tensioner weakens, and oil viscosity breaks down. Because the engine is an interference design, a slipped or jumped chain will cause the pistons to collide with the valves, resulting in catastrophic valve train and cylinder head destruction. The chain rattle on cold start is the definitive early warning sign, and ignoring it is essentially gambling with the entire engine. The 181 hp export calibration is mechanically identical to the 184 hp European variant; the timing system is subjected to the same stresses and fails in exactly the same manner.",
    
        injectionType: "Multi-point port fuel injection (Bosch ME 9.7 engine management), specifically calibrated for certain Asian and overseas markets with modified ignition maps and SAE net power correction. Unlike modern direct-injection engines, the M271 sprays fuel into the intake ports upstream of the valves, which means the intake valves do receive some degree of fuel-washing and are far less prone to the severe carbon buildup that plagues GDI engines. However, the system is still highly sensitive to fuel quality, vacuum leaks, and the condition of the intake manifold gaskets. The supercharged induction setup means any small air leak post-compressor will confuse the mass airflow readings, leading to erratic idle, lean spikes, and potential detonation. The export ECU calibration was adapted for regional fuel quality and emissions testing protocols, which slightly alters the power rating but does not change the injection hardware or its maintenance requirements.",
    
        engineLifespan: "180,000-250,000 km is realistic with strict proactive maintenance, though many examples fail far earlier due to timing chain neglect. The bottom end of the M271 is reasonably robust, but the engine's survival is almost entirely dependent on how early the timing chain issue is addressed. The Eaton supercharger is also a wear item that requires periodic oil servicing; if ignored, the bearings will fail and the unit will become noisy or seize. Once the chain and supercharger are properly maintained, the core engine can serve reliably. The 181 hp calibration does not materially alter mechanical lifespan compared to the 184 hp variant; the hardware, timing system, and supercharger are identical.",
    
        turboType: "Supercharged (Eaton M62 Roots-type positive displacement supercharger). The compressor is belt-driven directly from the crankshaft, providing instantaneous boost with zero lag and a very flat torque curve. It is a mechanically simple and robust design compared to turbochargers, but the internal bearings rely on a dedicated oil supply that degrades over time. The unit requires periodic oil changes using specific supercharger oil, not engine oil, and the small plug for this service is often overlooked by general mechanics. The M271.957 variant runs a higher boost pressure than the lower-output M271.946, which the Eaton unit handles within its design envelope but with slightly more thermal and mechanical stress on the bearings and rotor tolerances. The 181 hp and 184 hp variants share identical supercharger hardware.",
    
        ecuType: "Bosch ME 9.7 (Motor Electronics) with export market calibration. The ECU software is mapped to produce 181 hp rather than 184 hp, primarily to accommodate SAE net measurement corrections and regional fuel quality variations while maintaining safe knock margins. The physical hardware, wiring, and vulnerability to cam adjuster oil wicking are identical to the European variant. The export ignition maps may be slightly more conservative in certain load ranges to protect engine integrity in markets with variable fuel octane consistency.",
    
        euroStandard: "L3 / L4 / Regional Equivalent",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "210-220 hp is the practical safe ceiling on stock internals. The 1.8L block and the specific pistons used in the M271.957 are not massively over-engineered for extreme boost, and the supercharger itself reaches thermal and mechanical efficiency limits quickly. Attempting to extract significantly more power via smaller pulleys or aggressive remapping without addressing fueling, cooling, and intercooling will result in piston damage, excessive knock, or supercharger bearing failure. The 181 hp calibration offers marginally less aggressive factory timing, but the mechanical hard limits remain unchanged.",
    
