        "timingType": "Front-mounted single-row timing chain driving both intake and exhaust camshafts and the high-pressure fuel pump. The fleet variant uses identical OM 654.920 hardware to the standard 200 hp CLE 220 d. The 163 hp output is achieved purely through ECU software calibration with no mechanical changes whatsoever. The timing chain, camshafts, VVT actuators, and high-pressure fuel pump are the same components operating at reduced duty cycles. The lower power output actually reduces stress on the timing system, turbocharger, and fuel injection components, potentially improving longevity marginally compared to the 200 hp variant. The 48V ISG system operates identically. The front-mounted chain is accessible without transmission removal. The critical valvetrain vulnerability — premature roller bearing wear on the rocker arms — is identical to the 200 hp variant because the hardware is the same. The reduced cam phasing and injection loads may slightly delay wear onset but do not eliminate the fundamental maintenance sensitivity. Proactive oil changes at 10,000-15,000 km intervals with MB 228.51/229.51 low-ash synthetic diesel oil remain essential. The extended factory oil change interval is particularly dangerous on this variant because fleet vehicles often receive minimal maintenance attention.",
      
        "injectionType": "Fourth-generation Bosch common-rail direct injection with piezo-electric solenoid injectors operating at a maximum rail pressure of 2,700 bar. The fleet variant uses the exact same injectors, high-pressure pump, and rail as the 200 hp CLE 220 d. The 163 hp calibration operates the injectors at lower duty cycles and rail pressures, reducing cumulative stress on the nozzle orifices and the pump's internal components. This is beneficial for long-term injector and pump durability. The 2,700 bar system is identical in all hardware; only the ECU mapping limits output. The multi-way EGR system (cooled high-pressure and low-pressure circuits) operates at reduced flow rates compared to the 200 hp calibration, which may marginally reduce intake fouling rates over time. However, the fundamental vulnerability to poor fuel quality, water contamination, and extended oil change intervals remains identical. The injectors require the same protection through regular fuel filter changes (every 30,000 km) and consistent use of quality EN 590 diesel fuel. The fleet variant is an attractive candidate for remapping because identical hardware supports the full 200 hp output without any mechanical modification, offering an immediate and safe power increase if legally permissible.",
      
        "engineLifespan": "300,000-500,000+ km projected. The fleet variant's 163 hp calibration places less mechanical and thermal stress on the identical OM 654.920 hardware than the 200 hp variant. The lower power output, reduced turbocharger speeds, lower injection pressures, and gentler EGR rates all contribute to marginally slower wear progression. The 48V ISG system enables the same coasting and start-stop benefits. The steel pistons, forged crankshaft, Nanoslide-coated block, and robust bottom end are conservatively loaded at 163 hp. The most significant factor affecting longevity remains maintenance discipline: oil quality and change interval, fuel quality, AdBlue system care, and highway exercise for the DPF. Fleet vehicles are notoriously variable in maintenance quality; a well-maintained fleet car with highway-dominated use can achieve the upper end of the lifespan range, while a poorly maintained short-trip urban fleet car will face accelerated aftertreatment and valvetrain issues. The identical hardware to the 200 hp variant means that a remap to restore full output is feasible at any point, provided the engine has been properly maintained. The OM 654 is fundamentally a 500,000 km engine when treated correctly, regardless of the software calibration.",
      
        "turboType": "Single variable-geometry turbocharger (VGT) with aluminum turbine housing and water-cooled bearing system. The fleet variant uses the identical VGT turbocharger as the 200 hp CLE 220 d. The 163 hp calibration operates the VGT at lower boost pressures and reduced exhaust gas flows, which decreases thermal cycling stress on the turbine housing, bearings, and the VGT nozzle ring mechanism. The VGT actuator operates within a narrower range, potentially reducing mechanical wear on the linkage and vanes. The 48V ISG provides the same electric torque-fill during initial acceleration. The lower power demand means the turbocharger spends less time at maximum thermal load, which is beneficial for bearing longevity and oil stability in the bearing housing. The VGT vane sticking vulnerability from EGR carbon deposits remains present but may develop more slowly due to reduced EGR flow rates. The intercooler and boost plumbing are identical. After sustained highway driving, a brief idle cool-down is beneficial but less critical than on the higher-output petrol variants. The single VGT is a proven, durable design well-suited to the conservative 163 hp calibration.",
      
        "ecuType": "Multi-domain engine control with integrated 48V mild-hybrid management and SCR/DPF aftertreatment coordination. The fleet ECU is identical in hardware to the 200 hp variant but runs a conservative calibration limiting output to 163 hp and 400 Nm. The calibration is designed to meet favorable fleet taxation categories in certain Western European markets (particularly Benelux, France, and Germany) where vehicle tax and company car benefit-in-kind calculations are based on CO2 output and power rating. The 163 hp output often places the vehicle in a lower tax bracket, generating significant cost savings for fleet operators. The ECU manages the same functions: injection timing, 2,700 bar rail pressure control, VGT boost control, dual VVT, EGR rates, 48V ISG strategy, DPF regeneration scheduling, dual-stage SCR/AdBlue dosing, and NOx sensor monitoring. The ECU is encrypted but the fleet limitation is purely software; authorized dealers or tuners can restore the full 200 hp output. Flash updates are dealer-only via XENTRY. The software complexity is identical to the standard variant, and the same aftertreatment vulnerabilities apply. The fleet calibration's lower EGR and injection rates may produce slightly less particulate matter, marginally benefiting DPF loading intervals.",
      
        "euroStandard": "Euro 6d",
        "maxPowerStockInternals": "200 hp is immediately achievable by restoring the standard CLE 220 d calibration on identical hardware. Beyond 200 hp, the same 240-260 hp Stage 1/Stage 2 limits apply as to the standard OM 654.920 because the mechanical components are identical. The fleet variant is essentially a detuned standard engine, making it the most cost-effective path to 200+ hp if a remap is performed. The VGT, fuel system, and bottom end can handle the full 265 hp CLE 300 d output with supporting modifications, though this would stress the aftertreatment system and may affect emissions compliance.",
      
