  "timingType": "Front-mounted simplex (single-row) timing chain driving both intake and exhaust camshafts and the intermediate shaft for the ignition distributor. The M111.945 uses a relatively short, accessible front-mounted timing chain with hydraulic tensioning. The chain is adequately robust for the 136 hp output but is sensitive to oil quality and maintenance history. The M111 family is known for timing chain stretch if oil changes are neglected; the hydraulic tensioner can reach the end of its travel, causing a rattling noise on startup and potential chain jump. The dual overhead camshafts are driven directly by the chain, and the intermediate shaft (which replaced the traditional camshaft-driven distributor in earlier Mercedes engines) adds an additional sprocket to the chain path. The camshaft adjuster (variable valve timing on the intake cam) is controlled by a solenoid valve and is a known wear item that can leak oil and produce a characteristic rattle. The HFM (Hot Film Mass) injection system is standard on this later pre-facelift variant. The chain generally lasts 150,000-200,000 km with proper oil maintenance. A startup rattle that persists for more than 2-3 seconds is a warning sign of tensioner wear or chain stretch. Oil changes every 10,000-15,000 km with quality 10W-40 or 5W-40 synthetic oil are essential for chain longevity.",

  "injectionType": "Multi-port manifold injection with Bosch HFM (Hot Film Mass) airflow measurement and sequential fuel injection. The M111.945 uses a relatively simple and reliable port fuel injection system with four intake manifold-mounted injectors. This is a significant reliability advantage compared to modern direct injection engines: there is no high-pressure fuel pump, no piezo injector complexity, and no intake valve carbon buildup because fuel washes over the valve backs. The HFM airflow sensor (hot-film type) is a known failure point; contamination or aging causes incorrect air mass readings, leading to rough idle, poor acceleration, and lean/rich mixture faults. The injectors themselves are robust but can develop spray pattern degradation after 150,000+ km. The fuel pressure regulator is integrated into the fuel pump assembly or mounted on the fuel rail, depending on model year. The M111's injection system is straightforward to diagnose with basic OBD-II tools, and replacement parts are inexpensive and widely available. The wiring harness to the HFM sensor, injectors, and ignition coils is part of the infamous biodegradable wiring harness that plagued Mercedes engines of this era.",

  "engineLifespan": "250,000-400,000+ km projected. The M111 is a fundamentally robust engine with a cast-iron block and aluminum DOHC head. The 136 hp output is conservative for the 2.0L displacement, meaning mechanical stresses are moderate. The forged steel crankshaft and robust bottom end are durable. The primary threats to longevity are not the core engine but the peripheral systems: the biodegradable wiring harness (which can cause misfires, stalls, and ECU damage if insulation crumbles), the HFM sensor, the camshaft adjuster leaks, and the timing chain if neglected. The M111 operates at higher RPM and with less refinement than the smoother inline-6 engines that defined Mercedes' reputation, which earned it some criticism as 'unrefined' when new, but this does not affect durability. Well-maintained examples with replaced wiring harnesses, regular oil changes, and addressed cam adjuster leaks routinely exceed 300,000 km. The key is ensuring the wiring harness has been replaced or rebuilt; original biodegradable harnesses in 1997-2000 cars are virtually guaranteed to have failed or be on the verge of failure. Exclusively urban, short-trip use accelerates valve seal wear and oil consumption. Highway-driven cars with consistent maintenance achieve the upper end of the lifespan range with ease. This is a mechanically simple, overbuilt engine that rewards basic care.",

  "turboType": "Naturally aspirated. The M111.945 is a naturally aspirated 2.0L four-cylinder with no turbocharger or supercharger. The 136 hp output is achieved through displacement and moderate compression ratio alone. This eliminates all turbocharger-related complexity, failure modes, and maintenance concerns. The intake tract is simple: air filter, HFM sensor, throttle body, intake manifold. There is no intercooler, no boost plumbing, no wastegate, and no turbo bearing wear to monitor. The engine's breathing is limited by the naturally aspirated configuration, which explains the modest 136 hp output and the need for higher RPM to achieve peak torque (3700-4500 rpm). The simplicity is a reliability advantage: the engine is easy to work on, inexpensive to maintain, and has no forced-induction thermal stress. The trade-off is performance; the CLK 200 is leisurely by modern standards, requiring approximately 11 seconds to reach 100 km/h. For a daily driver prioritizing reliability and simplicity over speed, the absence of forced induction is a feature, not a bug.",

  "ecuType": "Bosch ME-SFI (Multi-point Electronic Sequential Fuel Injection) engine control unit. The M111.945 uses a relatively simple ECU architecture compared to modern Mercedes engines. The ME-SFI system manages sequential fuel injection, ignition timing, idle speed control, evaporative emissions, and diagnostic functions. The ECU communicates via OBD-II. The ECU itself is generally reliable, but the wiring harness connecting it to the engine sensors and actuators is the critical vulnerability. The biodegradable wiring insulation used by Mercedes from approximately 1992-1997 (with some spillover into 1998-2000) crumbles and flakes off, exposing bare wire. This causes short circuits, open circuits, erratic sensor readings, and can damage the ECU itself. The M111's ECU is not encrypted against tuning; basic remaps are possible, though the naturally aspirated configuration limits meaningful gains. The camshaft adjuster solenoid, HFM sensor, coolant temperature sensor, and throttle position sensor are the primary inputs. The ECU is mounted in the engine bay and can suffer from heat and moisture exposure over time. Replacement ECUs are available from scrap yards and specialists, and the ME-SFI system is well-understood by independent Mercedes mechanics. The simplicity of the ECU and its accessibility make this a far more serviceable engine than any modern Mercedes powertrain.",

  "euroStandard": "Euro 2 / Euro 3 (depending on market and exact production date)",
  "maxPowerStockInternals": "160-170 hp is achievable with conservative bolt-on modifications (intake, exhaust, camshaft) on stock internals. The M111 is not a high-performance engine in 136 hp trim, but the 2.0L block and head have headroom. The Kompressor (supercharged) variants of the M111 (M111.955/956) produce 163-192 hp on the same bottom end, confirming that the block and rotating assembly can handle significantly more than 136 hp. A naturally aspirated M111.945 cannot practically reach Kompressor power levels without forced induction, but mild porting, a performance camshaft, intake and exhaust upgrades, and an ECU remap can extract 150-160 hp reliably. The stock injectors and fuel system have adequate flow for moderate gains. The risk with any modification on a 25+ year old engine is that accumulated wear (piston rings, valve seals, bearings) may not tolerate increased loads. The most practical 'upgrade' for a CLK 200 is maintaining the engine in perfect health rather than chasing power, or swapping in a Kompressor engine if more performance is desired.",

