  "commonIssues": [
    "Biodegradable Wiring Harness Degradation: The same critical issue as the naturally aspirated M111. The engine wiring harness insulation crumbles and flakes, exposing bare copper wire. This causes misfires, hard starting, stalling, rough idle, and can short-circuit the ECU. The supercharger's bypass valve solenoid and the HFM sensor are particularly vulnerable to harness-related faults. The entire engine harness must be replaced or rebuilt with modern wire. This is mandatory on any 1997-2000 car that has not had its harness addressed.",
    "Eaton M62 Supercharger Oil Feed Line Failure: The small brass oil feed line near the oil filter housing is the supercharger's lifeline. If this line is blocked, kinked, leaking, or missing, the M62 bearings starve of oil and seize. A seized supercharger destroys the unit and can scatter debris into the intake. The oil feed line must be inspected at every service and replaced if showing corrosion or restriction.",
    "Supercharger Bearing Wear and Failure: The Eaton M62 internal bearings wear after 150,000-200,000 km. Symptoms include a progressively louder whine, grinding noise under load, and reduced boost pressure. A failed supercharger turns the 192 hp engine into a sluggish 8.5:1 compression NA motor. Rebuild or replacement is 800-1,500 EUR.",
    "Bypass Valve Malfunction: The electromagnetic bypass valve can stick open or closed. Stuck open causes no boost, sluggish performance, and no supercharger whine. Stuck closed causes excessive parasitic drag, poor fuel economy, and possible overboost conditions. The valve can be cleaned or replaced; the solenoid is a separate wear item.",
    "Supercharger Belt and Tensioner Wear: The dedicated supercharger drive belt stretches and cracks with age. The automatic tensioner can lose tension, causing belt slip, squealing, and inconsistent boost. The belt and tensioner should be inspected every 30,000 km and replaced as a set.",
    "HFM (Hot Film Mass) Sensor Failure: The HFM sensor is critical for the Kompressor engine because it must accurately measure the much larger air mass under boost. Contamination causes incorrect mixture, resulting in poor acceleration, lean or rich conditions under boost, and potential engine damage. Cleaning or replacement is essential.",
    "Intercooler Leaks and Heat Soak: The plastic end tanks on the air-to-air intercooler can crack, and the rubber hoses can leak. Leaks cause hissing, power loss, and reduced boost pressure. In hot climates, the intercooler can heat-soak during sustained driving, reducing performance until temperatures drop.",
    "Camshaft Adjuster (Variable Valve Timing) Leak and Rattle: The intake camshaft adjuster leaks oil and rattles, same as the NA variant. The supercharger's heat load may marginally accelerate adjuster seal aging.",
    "Timing Chain Stretch and Tensioner Wear: The duplex chain is more robust than the NA simplex chain but still stretches with neglected oil changes. The hydraulic tensioner reaches its travel limit, causing a rattle on startup. Preventive replacement at 150,000-180,000 km is recommended.",
    "Head Gasket External Leaks: The higher combustion pressures of the Kompressor engine make head gasket leaks between cylinders 1-2 or 3-4 more common than on NA variants. External coolant loss without oil contamination is the typical symptom.",
    "Oil Leaks (Valve Cover Gasket, Oil Pan): The aluminum valve cover gasket hardens and leaks, particularly around the spark plug tubes. The oil pan gasket weeps. The supercharger's oil feed line connection can also leak.",
    "Fuel Pump and Filter Degradation: The higher fuel demand under boost stresses the pump and filter. A weak pump causes lean conditions under load, leading to detonation, misfires, and potential piston damage. The filter should be replaced every 30,000 km.",
    "Ignition Coil Failure: The pencil-type ignition coils fail under the higher thermal and cylinder pressure loads of the Kompressor engine. Misfires under boost are often coil-related. The coils are stressed further if the wiring harness is degrading.",
    "Coolant Loss (Water Pump, Expansion Tank): The plastic expansion tank cracks. The water pump weeps from the bearing seal. These issues are common to all M111 engines but the Kompressor's higher operating temperatures may accelerate wear."
  ],

  "vulnerabilities": [
    "Wiring Harness as an Existential Threat: Same as the NA variant. The biodegradable wiring harness is a mandatory replacement item. On the Kompressor, harness failures can cause particularly dangerous symptoms: a shorted bypass valve solenoid can stick the valve closed, causing continuous boost and potential overboost; a shorted HFM sensor can cause extreme lean or rich mixtures under boost. The cost of replacement (400-800 EUR) is non-negotiable.",
    "Supercharger Oil Feed Line as a Single Point of Failure: The small brass oil feed line is the most critical single component on the entire engine. Its failure causes immediate and catastrophic supercharger seizure. The line is easily overlooked during routine maintenance because it is small and unobtrusive. Every owner must know its location and verify its condition at every oil change.",
    "Supercharger Bearing Failure and Replacement Cost: The Eaton M62 is a wear item. When it fails, the car loses the majority of its performance because the low 8.5:1 compression ratio makes NA operation sluggish. A supercharger rebuild or replacement is the single most expensive mechanical repair on this engine (800-1,500 EUR), far exceeding the cost of a timing chain or head gasket job.",
    "Lean Conditions Under Boost from Fuel System Weakness: The Kompressor demands significantly more fuel under boost. A clogged filter, weak pump, or partially blocked injector causes lean mixtures that can melt pistons or damage the head gasket. The symptoms (misfires under load, power loss) must be addressed immediately, not driven through.",
    "Detonation Sensitivity from Lower Compression and Timing: The 8.5:1 compression ratio is forgiving, but the higher boost and aggressive ignition timing on the 192 hp calibration make the engine sensitive to fuel octane. Using 91-92 RON fuel in a 192 hp Kompressor risks detonation under sustained load, particularly in hot weather. Premium fuel (95+ RON) is essential.",
    "Intercooler Heat Soak in Hot Climates: The air-to-air intercooler is adequate for temperate climates but can heat-soak in sustained high-speed driving or hot weather, reducing charge density and increasing the risk of detonation. An upgraded intercooler or water-methanol injection is beneficial for tuned cars in hot climates.",
    "Head Gasket Integrity Under Higher Cylinder Pressure: The head gasket is the same part number as the NA variant but must seal against significantly higher combustion pressures. This is a well-documented weak point on the M111 Kompressor. A failing head gasket may not immediately mix coolant and oil but can cause external coolant leaks and gradual compression loss between adjacent cylinders.",
    "Timing Chain Under Higher Dynamic Load: The duplex chain is more robust than the NA simplex chain but still operates under greater stress due to the higher torque output. Neglected oil changes accelerate chain wear. A jumped chain on a Kompressor is just as catastrophic as on the NA variant.",
    "ECU and Sensor Heat Sensitivity: The engine bay temperatures are higher on the Kompressor due to the supercharger's thermal output. The ECU, HFM sensor, and ignition coils are more stressed than on the NA variant. Ensuring the cooling system is in perfect condition is essential for managing under-hood temperatures.",
    "Market Value vs. Repair Cost Economics: The CLK 200 Kompressor commands a slight premium over the NA variant but is still an affordable classic. A supercharger failure plus a wiring harness replacement can approach or exceed the car's market value. Buyers must budget for these known vulnerabilities or accept a project car."
  ],

