  "yearRange": "2000-2003",

  "timingType": "Front-mounted duplex (double-row) timing chain driving both intake and exhaust camshafts and the intermediate shaft. The M111.956 EVO continues to use a robust front-mounted double-row chain, which is one of the reasons the M111 Kompressor family is still respected for durability. Compared with the earlier high-output M111.943 export version, the 163 hp EVO tune places noticeably less stress on the chain, guides, and tensioner. The hydraulic tensioner is generally durable, but chain noise on cold start after high mileage is still a warning sign that should not be ignored. The intake camshaft adjuster remains a known wear point; oil seepage and light rattling around the adjuster area are common as the engine ages. With regular oil changes using quality oil, the chain system usually lasts 180,000-250,000 km before preventive service becomes sensible. This is not a 'lifetime' chain in the real world, but it is stronger and less failure-prone than many later Mercedes petrol timing systems. The facelift-era EVO engine benefits from slightly better lubrication control and a milder torque curve than the more aggressive 192 hp pre-facelift export setup. Oil changes every 10,000-15,000 km are ideal if long-term chain, tensioner, and cam adjuster health are priorities.",

  "injectionType": "Multi-port manifold injection with Siemens ME engine management, sequential fuel injection, electronic throttle control, and a Roots-type supercharger with intercooling. The M111.956 EVO is a more modernized version of the earlier Kompressor layout, using a revised induction system and newer control logic to improve drivability, emissions behavior, and part-throttle efficiency. Unlike later direct-injection engines, this remains a port-injected setup, which means no high-pressure fuel pump, no direct-injection injector expense, and no intake valve carbon buildup from dry airflow alone. The fuel system is straightforward and durable if the fuel pump, filter, and pressure regulation remain healthy. Because this is a supercharged application, fuel delivery under load matters much more than on the naturally aspirated M111; weak fuel pressure can quickly create lean running under boost. The airflow metering and throttle control are also more refined than the early Bosch HFM-based Kompressor applications, giving the EVO engine a smoother, more modern response. The injectors are not especially problematic, but aging seals, vacuum leaks, or a weakening pump can make the engine feel flat and hesitant under load. Overall, the EVO fuel and control package is one of the main reasons the facelift CLK 200 Kompressor feels more polished than the pre-facelift supercharged cars.",

  "engineLifespan": "250,000-400,000+ km projected. The M111.956 EVO is one of the more durable supercharged four-cylinder engines Mercedes produced before the shift to smaller-displacement direct-injection turbo units. The cast-iron block, forged crankshaft, conservative 163 hp output, and relatively modest 230 Nm torque figure mean the base engine is not highly stressed. Compared with the earlier 192 hp M111.943 export variant, this EVO version trades outright performance for durability margin, smoother calibration, and lower thermal stress. The main threats to longevity are not usually the block or bottom end but the surrounding systems: supercharger wear, belt drive issues, air leaks in the boost tract, fuel delivery weakness under load, cooling-system age, and oil leaks from seals and gaskets. The facelift-era wiring is generally less notorious than the early biodegradable harness era, which is a real advantage over 1997-2000 cars. Well-maintained examples with timely oil changes, healthy ignition parts, and a sorted supercharger system can comfortably exceed 300,000 km. Neglected cars can become expensive because multiple medium-cost issues tend to appear together: belt drive, intercooler hoses, MAF, coils, mounts, oil leaks, and automatic transmission service. In terms of core engine longevity, the M111.956 is stronger than its modest output suggests.",

  "turboType": "Roots-type mechanically driven supercharger with intercooler. The M111.956 EVO does not use a turbocharger; it uses a belt-driven positive-displacement supercharger that delivers boost immediately from low RPM. This gives the facelift CLK 200 Kompressor its characteristic strong low-end response and smooth everyday drivability, without the lag that defined many early turbocharged petrol engines. The boost system is milder than the earlier 192 hp export tune, which is why the EVO feels smoother and less urgent but also tends to be easier on its own hardware. The supercharger assembly, bypass system, belt drive, and associated pipework are the main forced-induction wear points. Bearing noise, bypass faults, belt slip, and leaking intercooler hoses are far more common than catastrophic internal engine damage. Because the blower is mechanically driven, any parasitic or bearing issue is always present with engine speed; owners often notice changes in whine or load noise before outright failure. The system is robust when maintained, but it adds a permanent layer of complexity and cost that the naturally aspirated CLK 200 does not have. A healthy supercharged M111.956 should feel immediate, clean, and linear, not peaky or hesitant.",

  "ecuType": "Siemens ME engine control unit with integrated supercharger, electronic throttle, ignition, and fuel management. This is one of the defining differences between the facelift M111.956 EVO and the earlier Kompressor variants. The Siemens control strategy delivers smoother throttle transitions, better emissions calibration, and more refined drivability than the older setup. The ECU manages fuel delivery, ignition timing, throttle response, load calculation, bypass operation, and knock correction, and it is generally reliable if the charging system and wiring remain healthy. The engine responds well to professional remapping because the factory 163 hp calibration is conservative, but cheap tuning can quickly expose weaknesses in fuel supply, intake temperature control, and ignition components. One advantage of the EVO control system is that it tends to mask rough edges better in daily use, so some owners underestimate developing mechanical issues until they become more obvious. Intermittent hesitation, flat spots, or inconsistent boost response often trace back to sensor degradation, throttle adaptation, or air leaks rather than major ECU failure. As a whole, the Siemens-controlled EVO setup is a meaningful modernization over the pre-facelift Kompressor hardware and one of the reasons this engine is often seen as the better everyday choice.",

  "euroStandard": "Euro 3",
  "maxPowerStockInternals": "200-220 hp is achievable with conservative pulley, intake, exhaust, cooling, and ECU work on stock internals. The M111.956 EVO is tuned softly from the factory, and the same basic engine family has already proven that substantially more power is possible. A mild setup with a smaller pulley, well-managed fueling, colder plugs, and an efficient intercooler can deliver around 190-205 hp safely on a healthy engine. Pushing toward 220 hp is possible, but that is where charge temperature, fuel delivery, and supercharger efficiency start to matter more than the base engine strength. The bottom end itself is not the first limiting factor; the practical limits are usually the blower system, intake temperatures, ignition stability, and drivetrain age. The 722.6 automatic can handle this level of torque if serviced properly, and the manual gearbox also tolerates moderate upgrades. Beyond that point, the return on investment worsens quickly, and the car becomes more dependent on careful tuning and frequent maintenance. For most owners, a healthy stock or lightly tuned M111.956 is the sweet spot.",

