  "commonIssues": [
    "Supercharger Belt and Tensioner Wear: The accessory and blower drive components are routine wear items on the M111.956. Belt slip, chirping, squealing, inconsistent boost delivery, or visible cracking are signs the system needs attention. A weak tensioner can make the car feel softer than expected even when the engine itself is healthy.",
    "Bypass Valve and Boost Control Problems: The Roots-type supercharger uses a bypass system for part-throttle efficiency and smoother drivability. If the bypass sticks open, the engine feels flat and noticeably down on torque; if it sticks or behaves inconsistently, throttle response becomes uneven and fuel economy suffers.",
    "MAF / Air Metering Degradation: The EVO engine is smoother than the earlier Kompressor versions, but it still depends heavily on accurate air measurement. A tired mass-air sensor causes hesitation, poor throttle response, weak pull under load, and inconsistent fuel trims. Many 'slow' M111.956 cars simply need air-metering and vacuum-leak diagnosis.",
    "Intercooler Hose and Intake Leak Issues: Aging rubber couplers, boost hoses, and plastic intake connections leak with age. This causes hissing, soft boost response, mixture correction issues, and a general feeling that the car is weaker than factory output would suggest.",
    "Ignition Coil and Plug Fatigue: The supercharged load places more stress on ignition components than the naturally aspirated M111. Coil weakness often appears first under load, where the car may stumble, hesitate, or misfire only during stronger acceleration. Spark plugs should be changed on time and with the correct heat range.",
    "Camshaft Adjuster Leak and Rattle: The intake cam adjuster area can develop oil leaks and light noise with age. This is not unique to the EVO version, but it remains a well-known M111 family wear point.",
    "Oil Leaks from Valve Cover and Front Seals: The valve cover gasket, front crank seal, and other age-related seals begin to seep as these cars get older. Most of these are nuisance leaks at first, but they should not be ignored because oil contamination shortens belt life and can damage ignition components.",
    "Cooling System Aging: Expansion tanks, thermostat housings, pumps, hoses, and old radiators become brittle or weak with age. The Kompressor engine is not especially fragile, but like any supercharged petrol engine it does not appreciate overheating or marginal coolant flow.",
    "Fuel Pump Fatigue Under Load: A weak pump may still let the engine idle and cruise normally, but the car can feel starved or hesitant at higher load. On a supercharged engine this matters more than on an NA car, because high-load fueling must remain stable.",
    "Engine Mount Collapse: Hydraulic mounts soften or fail with age, allowing more vibration into the cabin and more drivetrain movement on load changes. This can make the engine feel rougher than it really is.",
    "722.6 Automatic Conductor Plate / Connector Leaks: On automatic cars, the gearbox itself is usually durable, but the electrical connector and conductor-plate related issues remain classic 722.6 concerns. Fluid migration into the wiring can create shift quality problems or limp mode.",
    "Rust and Age-Related Chassis Costs: While not an engine fault, rust and deferred chassis maintenance often become the real ownership cost driver on facelift CLKs. A good M111.956 in a rusty or neglected shell is still a bad buy."
  ],

  "vulnerabilities": [
    "Forced-Induction Dependency: The M111.956 is a mild Kompressor engine, but it still depends heavily on the blower system for its character. If the supercharger, bypass system, or boost plumbing is not working correctly, the engine feels much slower than its numbers suggest. Owners sometimes mistake this for 'old car sluggishness' rather than a solvable fault.",
    "Air Leak Sensitivity: The EVO setup is more refined than earlier Kompressor versions, but that also means it is very sensitive to vacuum and boost leaks. A small leak can create poor throttle response, inconsistent fueling, and a vague, disappointing driving feel without triggering a dramatic failure mode.",
    "Ignition Margin Under Load: Because this is a supercharged petrol engine, marginal plugs or coils show up faster than on the naturally aspirated M111. A car that feels almost normal in light driving can still have a real ignition weakness under boost. That can easily be misdiagnosed as a fueling or transmission problem.",
    "Heat Management on Older Examples: The 163 hp tune is conservative, but any aging supercharged engine still generates more thermal stress than the NA equivalent. Old coolant parts, tired intercooler hoses, dirty radiators, or cheap oil shorten the life of everything around the engine even if the block itself stays healthy.",
    "Cheap Tuning Risk: The EVO engine responds well to light tuning, which makes it attractive to owners chasing easy power. Poor remaps, undersized fueling, wrong spark plugs, or excessive pulley reduction quickly erode the reliability advantage of the stock setup. A badly tuned M111.956 is far more troublesome than a standard one.",
    "Automatic Transmission Neglect: The 722.6 is strong enough for this engine, but old fluid, conductor plate issues, and connector leaks create secondary costs that many owners ignore because the gearbox often 'still works' until it doesn't. The engine may be fine while the drivetrain lets the ownership experience down.",
    "Age-Stacked Maintenance: The biggest risk with these cars is not one fatal flaw but the stacking of multiple medium-size jobs at once: mounts, coils, hoses, leaks, MAF, belt drive, cooling parts, gearbox service, and rust work. Buyers expecting one simple fix are often surprised.",
    "Parts Quality Variation: The M111.956 itself is straightforward, but low-quality aftermarket ignition, air-metering, and rubber parts can make diagnosis frustrating. These engines reward OE-quality service parts and punish bargain-basement components.",
    "Underestimated Ownership Costs: Because the facelift CLK 200 Kompressor is not exotic and often appears affordable, buyers sometimes assume it will be cheap to run like a basic naturally aspirated four-cylinder. In reality it is still a Mercedes coupe with supercharger hardware, automatic gearbox complexity, and 20+ years of aging components.",
    "Market Value vs. Repair Logic: Even when repairs are rational mechanically, they are not always rational economically. A well-sorted M111.956 car is worth preserving; a neglected one can quickly exceed its market value in catch-up work."
  ],

