    "commonIssues": [
      "Supercharger Belt and Tensioner Wear: The M62 blower depends on a healthy belt drive. A tired tensioner or cracked belt causes slip, noise, inconsistent boost, and a softer-than-normal driving feel.",
      "Bypass Valve Problems: If the bypass sticks open, the engine feels flat and underpowered; if it behaves erratically, throttle response and fuel economy both suffer.",
      "Boost Leaks from Hoses and Couplers: Aging intake seals, intercooler hoses, and rubber couplers leak with age, causing hissing, weak pull, unstable fueling, and disappointing real-world performance.",
      "MAF / Air Metering Degradation: A tired airflow meter causes hesitation, poor throttle response, weak top-end pull, and odd fuel-trim behavior. Many slow CLK 230 Kompressors simply need proper air-metering diagnosis.",
      "Ignition Coil and Spark Plug Weakness Under Load: Misfires often appear only during boost or heavier acceleration. Wrong heat-range plugs or cheap coils make the engine feel rough and lazy.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster Leak and Rattle: The intake cam adjuster can seep oil and become noisy with age, just as on other M111 variants.",
      "Oil Leaks from Valve Cover and Front Seals: Valve cover gaskets, front crank seals, and other age-related seals begin to seep, eventually contaminating belts and ignition components.",
      "Cooling System Age: Expansion tanks, water pumps, thermostat housings, hoses, and radiators all age out. The engine itself is tough, but overheating is still unacceptable on a supercharged petrol car.",
      "Fuel Pump or Filter Weakness: Under load, weak fuel supply can flatten performance and create risky lean running conditions.",
      "Engine Mount Collapse: Old hydraulic mounts make the engine feel harsher and transmit more vibration than it naturally produces.",
      "722.6 Electrical Connector and Conductor Plate Issues: Automatic cars commonly suffer fluid migration through the connector, causing shift quality issues or limp mode.",
      "Age-Related Wiring and Connector Problems: Pre-facelift Mercedes electrical aging affects sensors, insulation, and connectors, and intermittent drivability faults can result."
    ],

    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Air and Boost Leak Sensitivity: The M111.973 can lose a surprising amount of real performance from very ordinary leaks. A car that feels merely 'old' is often actually down on boost or suffering from a metering problem.",
      "Forced-Induction Wear Stacking: The supercharger system itself is durable, but belt drive, bypass hardware, hoses, clamps, and air-metering all age together. That means several medium-cost repairs can arrive at once.",
      "Ignition Stability Under Load: A marginal ignition system may behave acceptably at light throttle but break down under boost. This makes faults easy to misread as fuel or gearbox issues.",
      "Cooling System Neglect: The engine tolerates mileage very well, but it does not tolerate overheating. An old cooling system can turn a strong engine into an expensive project very quickly.",
      "Cheap Tuning Risk: Smaller pulleys and generic remaps are common on these engines, but poor fueling, weak intercooling, and old ignition parts erase the reliability margin very fast.",
      "Automatic Transmission Neglect: The 722.6 is strong enough for the torque, but many cars have overdue fluid, connector, or conductor-plate issues that drag down the ownership experience.",
      "Age-Stacked Ownership Costs: The main risk is not a single fatal design flaw but the accumulation of belts, hoses, sensors, mounts, leaks, rust, and transmission service on a 20-plus-year-old Mercedes coupe.",
      "Parts Quality Variation: These engines react badly to low-grade aftermarket MAFs, coils, seals, and rubber parts. OE-quality components make a huge difference in drivability.",
      "Market Value vs. Repair Economics: Mechanically, many repairs are worth doing; financially, they are not always worth doing on a neglected low-value example."
    ],

