    "timingType": "Front-mounted duplex (double-row) timing chain driving both camshafts and the intermediate shaft. Mechanically, the M111.975 is extremely close to the M111.973, and its timing system shares the same core strengths: a stout duplex chain, durable front-drive layout, and good service life when oil quality is maintained. The slightly softer North American ignition and emissions-oriented calibration does not materially change the chain hardware or its wear pattern. As with the European pre-facelift Kompressor engines, the key warning signs are persistent cold-start rattle, aging chain guides, and a tired hydraulic tensioner after high mileage. The intake camshaft adjuster remains a familiar M111 wear point, especially for oil seepage and occasional light noise. In real use, the timing system is stronger than many later Mercedes petrol engines and is not usually the first thing that scares experienced buyers. Preventive attention still matters, because ignored chain noise on any interference engine is bad practice. Regular 10,000-15,000 km oil changes with quality oil remain the best protection for the chain, tensioner, and adjuster assembly.",

    "injectionType": "Multi-port manifold injection with Bosch ME-SFI engine management, sequential fuel injection, and Roots-type supercharging. The M111.975 North American version uses the same essential forced-induction architecture as the European M111.973 but with market-specific calibration choices for emissions compliance, ignition safety margin, and power reporting standards. The 192 hp figure is functionally a rounding and market-rating difference rather than a meaningful hardware downgrade. The port-injected layout remains one of the engine's biggest long-term strengths: no direct-injection hardware, no expensive high-pressure pump, and no typical intake-valve carbon pattern associated with later DI motors. The system still depends heavily on healthy fuel delivery under load, because weak fuel pressure on a supercharged petrol engine creates lean running risk faster than on an NA setup. The airflow and load control side must also remain tight; North American cars are no less sensitive to vacuum leaks, MAF degradation, and boost leaks than European ones. In healthy condition, the system is smooth, strong in the mid-range, and easier to keep running well than many newer Mercedes petrol engines. A tired M111.975 usually feels flat rather than obviously broken, which is why so many need proper diagnosis instead of random parts replacement.",

    "engineLifespan": "250,000-400,000+ km projected. The M111.975 shares the same strong 2.3L cast-iron M111 Kompressor foundation as the European M111.973 and is equally capable of very high mileage when cared for properly. The North American and Canadian calibration is slightly more conservative on ignition behavior and market-rated output, which does no harm at all to durability and may slightly reduce thermal stress in real use. The long block itself is not the common source of financial pain; surrounding systems are. Supercharger wear, belt drive, air leaks, ignition parts, cooling hardware, oil seepage, mounts, and automatic-transmission maintenance are the usual ownership costs. In North American cars, age-related rubber, emissions plumbing, and sensor drift often play a bigger role in drivability complaints than any internal engine wear. This engine's reputation for durability is deserved, especially compared with many later supercharged and turbocharged Mercedes four-cylinders. A well-kept M111.975 can feel strong and refined at very high mileage. A neglected one usually remains mechanically recoverable, but the cost of catching up all the support systems can easily exceed the car's market value.",

    "turboType": "Eaton M62 Roots-type mechanically driven supercharger with intercooling. The North American M111.975 uses the same broad positive-displacement supercharger concept that defines the European 2.3L Kompressor cars: immediate boost, no lag, and a torque-rich response from low RPM. The slightly softer rating does not mean the hardware is fundamentally different in character. It still feels substantially stronger in real driving than the naturally aspirated CLK 230 would have, and its 280 Nm torque figure gives the car relaxed but genuinely brisk everyday performance. The blower system still relies on healthy belt drive, intake sealing, bypass operation, and clean airflow metering. Bearing noise, belt slip, and intake leaks remain the most common age-related forced-induction issues. The system is robust when maintained, but as on all older Kompressor engines it creates a layer of permanent complexity that buyers of the simpler NA engines do not face. A healthy M111.975 should feel eager, smooth, and immediate. If it feels dull, there is usually a service issue to solve rather than a fundamental design limitation.",

    "ecuType": "Bosch ME-SFI engine control unit with North American market-specific ignition, load, and emissions calibration. The M111.975 is best understood as the North American sibling of the M111.973 rather than a fundamentally different engine. The key distinction is calibration: stricter ignition timing behavior, emissions compliance, and market-specific output rating conventions. That is why the nominal figure is 192 hp rather than 193 hp, while torque remains effectively identical. The ECU still manages airflow, ignition, fuel, throttle behavior, knock control, and bypass operation in a relatively simple and durable package. In service, the same principles apply as on the European engine: most apparent 'ECU issues' are really airflow, leak, ignition, or fuel-delivery issues. The system is remap-capable, and many owners restore a slightly sharper response with careful tuning, but that only works well if the intake tract, ignition parts, and fueling are genuinely healthy. The Bosch management remains a strong point because it is old enough to be understandable and modern enough to make the car feel civilized. For daily usability, the M111.975 calibration is smoother than many buyers expect from an older supercharged four-cylinder.",

    "euroStandard": "OBD-II / Federal-California emissions specification (market-dependent)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220-235 hp is achievable with conservative pulley, intake, exhaust, fueling, and ECU work on stock internals. Because the M111.975 is mechanically so close to the M111.973, its practical tuning ceiling is very similar. The slightly softer stock calibration actually means there is a little easy headroom in the factory tune before hardware becomes the limiting factor. A healthy car with a conservative smaller pulley, proper fueling, good plugs, and a quality remap can move safely into the low-220 hp range. Beyond that, intake temperatures, ignition quality, belt-drive stability, and old drivetrain components matter more than the basic engine strength. The bottom end is not the problem; supporting hardware is. North American pump fuel quality and octane availability should always be considered before aggressive tuning, especially in hot climates. A lightly tuned M111.975 can be very satisfying, but the best gains usually come from restoring lost factory performance first. Many owners are surprised how much quicker the car feels after simply fixing leaks, ignition weakness, and overdue maintenance.",

