      "commonIssues": [
        "Ignition Coil Destruction from Neglected Spark Plugs: The most important and well-known issue on the M112. Each cylinder has two spark plugs fed by dual-output ignition coils. If the plugs are not replaced on schedule, the coils work harder and fail prematurely. This is expensive because there are 12 plugs and 6 coils to service.",
        "HFM (Hot Film Mass) Sensor Failure: The airflow sensor is critical for correct fueling. Contamination causes hesitation, poor acceleration, rich running, and fault codes. Cleaning can help; replacement is often necessary on high-mileage cars.",
        "Oil Cooler Leaks: The oil cooler mounted near the oil filter housing develops leaks at the gasket and sealing surfaces, causing oil loss into the coolant or externally onto the engine.",
        "Intake Manifold Gasket Oil Leak: A known M112 issue where oil leaks past the intake manifold gasket and can enter the coolant system or create external oil seepage.",
        "Cylinder Head Gasket External Weep: Especially at the rear of the engine, the head gasket can develop an external oil leak that is slow but persistent.",
        "Valve Cover Gasket Leaks: The aluminum valve covers tend to weep oil with age, particularly into the spark plug tubes, which can cause misfires and coil contamination.",
        "Cooling System Aging: Expansion tanks, water pumps, thermostats, hoses, and radiators all age out. The aluminum V6 does not tolerate overheating well.",
        "Engine Mount Collapse: Hydraulic mounts soften and leak, causing excessive vibration and harsh driveline behavior.",
        "Oil Consumption from Valve Seals: With very high mileage, valve stem seals can harden and allow oil consumption, visible as blue smoke on cold starts.",
        "Camshaft Position Sensor Failure: The V6 uses camshaft position sensors that can fail with age, causing rough running, hard starting, and fault codes.",
        "Crankshaft Position Sensor Failure: A common Mercedes issue of this era; failure causes stalling, no-start, or erratic running.",
        "Exhaust Flex Pipe and Catalytic Converter Aging: The front exhaust flex pipe cracks with age, and the catalytic converter can clog after very high mileage.",
        "722.6 Automatic Connector and Conductor Plate Issues: On automatic cars, the familiar 722.6 electrical and fluid-migration problems apply.",
        "Oil Leaks from Rear Main Seal and Front Crank Seal: Age-related seal leakage is common and can become significant if ignored."
      ],
  
      "vulnerabilities": [
        "Dual-Spark Maintenance Trap: The 12-plug, 6-coil ignition system is the single biggest maintenance cost driver. Neglecting plugs destroys coils, and replacing both is expensive. This is not optional maintenance; it is mandatory.",
        "HFM Sensor as a Drivability Critical Point: A failed HFM sensor makes the V6 run poorly in ways that are easy to misdiagnose as transmission or fuel-system issues.",
        "Oil Leak Multiplicity: The all-aluminum construction and gasket sealing can develop multiple leak points simultaneously: oil cooler, valve covers, intake manifold, head gasket, front and rear seals. The cumulative cost can be significant.",
        "Cooling System Sensitivity: The aluminum block and heads expand and contract at different rates than iron components. Any overheating event or cooling-system weakness can stress head gasket sealing and warp components.",
        "Age-Stacked Ownership Costs: Like all 20-plus-year-old Mercedes, the CLK 320 accumulates medium-cost jobs: mounts, ignition, cooling, leaks, sensors, transmission service, and rubber aging.",
        "Market Value vs. Repair Economics: The M112.940 is a genuinely good engine, but a neglected CLK 320 can need more in catch-up repairs than its current market value supports.",
        "Transmission Neglect: The 722.6 automatic is strong but requires disciplined maintenance. Fluid migration through the electrical connector is a known failure path.",
        "Parts Quality for Ignition: Cheap aftermarket coils and plugs on a dual-spark engine often create more problems than they solve. OE-quality ignition parts are essential.",
        "Intake Manifold and Oil Migration: Oil leaking into the intake tract or coolant from manifold gasket failure can create a cascade of secondary issues.",
        "Rust and Chassis Deterioration: Even with an excellent engine, a rusty or structurally tired CLK 320 is not a rational restoration candidate."
      ],
  
