        "commonIssues": [
          "Ignition Coil Destruction from Neglected Spark Plugs: The dual-spark system requires all 16 plugs to be replaced on schedule. Neglected plugs force the 8 dual-output coils to work harder, causing premature and expensive coil failure.",
          "Valve Cover Gasket Leaks: The aluminum valve covers weep oil with age, particularly into the spark plug tubes, causing misfires and coil contamination. This is one of the most common M113 issues.",
          "Oil Cooler Leaks: The oil cooler mounted near the oil filter housing develops leaks at gaskets and sealing surfaces, causing oil loss into the coolant or externally.",
          "Rear Main Seal Leakage: The crankshaft rear main seal weeps oil with high mileage and age, causing drips between the engine and transmission.",
          "Intake Manifold Gasket Oil Leak: Oil can leak past the intake manifold gasket, creating external seepage or oil migration into the coolant.",
          "HFM (Hot Film Mass) Sensor Failure: Contamination causes hesitation, poor acceleration, rich running, and fault codes. Cleaning can help; replacement is often necessary.",
          "Engine Mount Collapse: Hydraulic mounts soften and leak, transmitting excessive vibration and harshness into the cabin.",
          "Oil Consumption from Valve Stem Seals: With very high mileage, hardened valve stem seals allow oil to seep into the combustion chambers, visible as blue smoke on cold starts.",
          "Cooling System Aging: Expansion tanks, water pumps, thermostats, hoses, and radiators all age out. The aluminum V8 does not tolerate overheating.",
          "Camshaft Position Sensor Failure: Can cause rough running, hard starting, and fault codes.",
          "Crankshaft Position Sensor Failure: A common Mercedes issue of this era causing stalling, no-start, or erratic running.",
          "Exhaust Flex Pipe and Catalytic Converter Aging: The front exhaust flex pipe cracks with age, and catalytic converters can clog after very high mileage.",
          "BAS/ESP Warning Light: A common fault on W208 cars, often caused by a bad brake light switch, low battery, or wheel speed sensor issues.",
          "722.6 Automatic Connector and Conductor Plate Issues: The familiar 722.6 electrical and fluid-migration problems apply.",
          "Throttle Position Sensor Wear: The electronic throttle body or its position sensor can wear, causing erratic idle, hesitation, and fault codes."
        ],
    
        "vulnerabilities": [
          "Dual-Spark Maintenance Trap: The 16-plug, 8-coil ignition system is the single most expensive routine maintenance item. This is absolutely mandatory on schedule.",
          "Oil Leak Multiplicity: Multiple leak points commonly develop with age: valve covers, oil cooler, rear main seal, front crank seal, intake manifold gasket.",
          "HFM Sensor as a Drivability Critical Point: A failed HFM sensor causes significant mixture errors on a V8 that moves large volumes of air.",
          "Cooling System Sensitivity: The all-aluminum construction is less forgiving of overheating than iron-block engines. Old cooling parts are a serious risk.",
          "Age-Stacked Ownership Costs: Like all 20-plus-year-old Mercedes, the CLK 430 accumulates medium-cost jobs: mounts, ignition, cooling, leaks, sensors, transmission service.",
          "Market Value vs. Repair Economics: A neglected example can need more in repairs than its current market value supports.",
          "Cheap Aftermarket Ignition Parts: Low-grade coils and plugs on a dual-spark V8 create cascading drivability problems.",
          "Rust and Chassis Deterioration: Even with an excellent V8, a rusty or structurally compromised CLK 430 is not a rational restoration candidate.",
          "Fuel Costs: The 4.3L V8 is thirsty, especially in city driving. Fuel economy is a genuine ownership consideration.",
          "Transmission Neglect: The 722.6 is strong enough for the torque but requires disciplined maintenance."
        ],
    
