          "timingType": "One duplex roller timing chain per cylinder bank, driven from the crankshaft to the SOHC camshafts. The late-production M113.984 keeps the same fundamentally robust timing architecture as the earlier M113.981, with no dramatic change in service philosophy. The M113 family remains one of Mercedes-AMG's most dependable naturally aspirated V8 platforms because the chain layout is short, the valvetrain is mechanically simple, and the engine does not rely on extreme RPM to make power. The late variant may benefit from small running production refinements in guides, seals, and calibration strategy, but from an ownership perspective it behaves like the same durable M113 AMG formula. Cold-start noise remains the main symptom to monitor for chain or tensioner wear on very high-mileage cars. In practice, most late CLK 55 AMG engines will age out through peripheral maintenance needs long before the timing system becomes a primary concern. Frequent oil service remains the best protection. This is still a timing setup that specialists trust far more than many later AMG V8 systems.",
      
          "injectionType": "Sequential multi-port manifold injection with Bosch ME 2.8 engine management, electronic throttle, and dual-spark ignition. The M113.984 is best understood as a late-production refinement of the same 5.4L naturally aspirated AMG V8 concept rather than a fundamentally different engine. It retains port injection, which is excellent news for long-term reliability because there is no direct-injection complexity, no high-pressure pump, and no severe intake carbon issue. The fuel system remains robust and predictable, and the engine's enormous torque comes from displacement and combustion efficiency rather than complicated boost strategy. The dual-spark layout still requires disciplined service because there are 16 plugs and 8 coils involved, but the overall system is mature and well-proven. The later variant typically feels slightly more polished in low-speed behavior and emissions control, though the mechanical sensation is essentially identical to the early engine. Healthy examples deliver crisp throttle response, very strong mid-range, and outstanding drivability. As before, most running issues come from age, air metering, ignition wear, or vacuum-related faults rather than the core injection hardware.",
      
          "engineLifespan": "300,000-450,000+ km projected. The late M113.984 shares the same reputation for exceptional durability as the early M113.981 because the underlying engine is still the same large-capacity, low-stress naturally aspirated AMG V8. If anything, late-production engines are often slightly easier to live with simply because they benefit from incremental production maturity and are usually newer cars overall. The 347 hp output remains conservative for a 5.4L V8, and the engine's enormous torque is produced without straining the internal hardware. This is why the M113 AMG is so respected: it feels exotic in character but not in fragility. Internal failures are uncommon when oil level, cooling health, and basic maintenance are managed properly. Ownership costs still come from the familiar cluster of support-system work: coil and spark plug service, valve cover leaks, MAF faults, coolant system renewal, mounts, transmission upkeep, and general rubber aging. A good M113.984 is capable of extremely high mileage while still feeling strong and civilized. The late engine remains one of the best classic AMG powerplants for buyers who want genuine performance without constant mechanical anxiety.",
      
          "turboType": "Naturally aspirated. Like the early variant, the M113.984 makes its performance without any turbocharger or supercharger. The powertrain relies on displacement, efficient breathing, and AMG-specific calibration rather than forced induction. This gives the late CLK 55 AMG its defining feel: immediate throttle response, a huge wall of low-end torque, and a very clean, linear rise in power. There is no lag, no blower whine, and no boosted heat stress to manage. For owners, this means fewer high-cost systems and fewer tuning-related weak points. The naturally aspirated format is one of the major reasons the M113 AMG remains so well loved among specialists. It offers nearly everything people want from an AMG V8—sound, torque, and effortless speed—without the mechanical complication of later supercharged or turbocharged cars.",
      
          "ecuType": "Bosch ME 2.8 engine control unit with dual-spark ignition management, electronic throttle control, and AMG-specific calibration. The late M113.984 uses the same overall control philosophy as the earlier engine, but late-production calibration tends to feel slightly smoother in cold-start and transient throttle behavior. The ECU is still conservative, durable, and well matched to both the engine and the AMG-calibrated 722.6 automatic transmission. It delivers clean idle behavior, strong throttle response, and excellent real-world drivability. ECU hardware failures remain uncommon; most issues blamed on the control unit are usually caused by airflow metering, throttle body wear, crank or cam sensors, or ignition faults. The engine can be remapped for mild gains, but the stock calibration is already very complete. In workshop terms, this is a mature and well-understood control system that supports the M113's reputation for painless long-term ownership. It makes the late CLK 55 AMG feel fast without ever feeling unruly.",
      
          "euroStandard": "Euro 3",
          "maxPowerStockInternals": "390-410 hp is realistic with intake, exhaust, ECU tuning, and supporting maintenance on stock internals. The late M113.984 offers essentially the same tuning headroom as the earlier M113.981 because both are fundamentally the same AMG 5.4L naturally aspirated engine family. Small production refinements do not materially change the safe tuning ceiling. Mild OEM+ modifications are the most sensible route because they preserve the smoothness and reliability that make the car special. High-300-horsepower output is achievable without major sacrifice if the cooling system, ignition system, and transmission are healthy. Beyond around 400 hp, naturally aspirated tuning becomes disproportionately expensive. Forced-induction conversions are possible, but they move the car away from its factory character and create much greater drivetrain stress. The stock internals themselves remain very strong. For most owners, restoring full original performance is more rewarding than chasing large dyno numbers.",
      
