          "timingType": "One duplex roller timing chain per cylinder bank, driven from the crankshaft to the SOHC camshafts. The USA-spec M113.981 uses the same exceptionally durable timing hardware as the global CLK 55 AMG engine. The slight power difference is due to SAE net measurement and market calibration, not to weaker mechanical internals. The timing system remains one of the strongest aspects of the engine, especially compared with many later AMG V8s. Regular synthetic oil service is still critical because long intervals accelerate tensioner and guide wear on any high-output aluminum V8. In real ownership, timing problems are rare relative to the number of miles many American M113 cars accumulate. Cold-start rattle remains the main warning sign to monitor. As with all M113 AMG engines, the timing system usually lasts far longer than the seals, mounts, and ancillary components around it.",
      
          "injectionType": "Sequential multi-port manifold injection with Bosch ME 2.8 management, electronic throttle, dual-spark ignition, and North American emissions calibration. The USA CLK 55 AMG uses the same basic M113.981 hardware as the global car, but its quoted 342 hp reflects SAE net power rating rather than a materially weaker engine. Port injection remains a major long-term advantage because it avoids the complexity of later direct-injection AMG engines. The fuel system is robust, with strong injector reliability and excellent drivability when the airflow metering and ignition system are healthy. The dual-spark arrangement still means 16 spark plugs and 8 coils, which is the single most important routine maintenance burden. North American emissions hardware and calibration can make the car slightly more sensitive to certain sensor and EVAP-related faults, but mechanically it is still the same famously durable M113 AMG formula. Properly sorted cars feel immediate, smooth, and extremely muscular. Most drivability issues still trace to MAF accuracy, ignition wear, vacuum aging, or throttle body concerns rather than to the injectors themselves.",
      
          "engineLifespan": "300,000-450,000+ km projected. The USA-spec M113.981 is every bit as durable as the global version because the 342 hp rating is a standards difference, not a true loss of engine strength. The 5.4L AMG V8 remains a low-stress, high-displacement performance engine with a very strong bottom end and excellent long-term reputation. In North American ownership, these engines often cover high mileage because they suit highway use so well and tolerate regular use better than many collectible performance cars. The usual threats remain the same: aging ignition hardware, cooling-system neglect, oil leaks, engine mounts, airflow metering issues, and deferred transmission service. The long block itself is rarely the expensive part. Even after high mileage, many USA M113 AMG engines still feel remarkably healthy if the maintenance history is strong. This is one of the reasons the CLK 55 AMG remains such a respected used AMG in the American market. It delivers real hand-built AMG presence without the fragility many buyers fear.",
      
          "turboType": "Naturally aspirated. The USA M113.981 relies on displacement, breathing efficiency, and calibration rather than boost to make its performance. The 342 hp SAE net rating translates to essentially the same real-world experience as the 347 hp metric figure used elsewhere. On the road, the engine feels huge, immediate, and effortless. There is no lag and no supercharger complexity, just a broad, powerful wave of V8 torque that makes the car devastatingly effective in real traffic. The naturally aspirated layout is also a huge benefit for long-term reliability and serviceability. There are fewer expensive systems to fail, less under-bonnet heat concentration than on later forced-induction AMG cars, and a very straightforward performance character. For North American buyers who want an AMG that can be driven regularly, this is one of the most sensible V8 choices of its era.",
      
          "ecuType": "Bosch ME 2.8 engine control unit with dual-spark ignition control, electronic throttle, sequential port injection, OBD-II compliance, and North American emissions calibration. The USA-specific calibration is designed around federal and state emissions requirements, but it still delivers the full AMG character. The small difference in published horsepower is largely a rating methodology issue rather than a meaningful on-road performance change. The ECU manages the engine with excellent smoothness and strong part-throttle behavior, while also supporting OBD-II diagnostics that make fault finding easier in the North American service environment. ECU failure itself is uncommon; sensors, MAF drift, ignition issues, throttle wear, and wiring age are more common causes of trouble. The stock calibration is already very good, and mild tuning works best when all baseline maintenance is perfect. In daily use, the Bosch control system makes the USA CLK 55 AMG feel refined, tractable, and very modern for its age. It is one of the quiet strengths of the car.",
      
          "euroStandard": "EPA / LEV / ULEV depending on production year and market calibration",
          "maxPowerStockInternals": "390-410 hp is realistic with intake, exhaust, ECU tuning, and supporting maintenance on stock internals. The USA-spec M113.981 has the same practical tuning ceiling as the global engine because the hardware is fundamentally the same. A healthy North American car with premium fuel, proper airflow metering, and a conservative tune can gain useful power without sacrificing reliability. As with the global version, high-300-horsepower output is the sensible naturally aspirated target. Beyond that, cost rises sharply and driveline demands increase. The engine itself remains robust enough for more, but transmission condition and supporting-system health become the real limiting factors. For road use, the stock or mildly tuned USA car is already more than fast enough. The real value lies in preserving the engine's effortless AMG character rather than chasing headline dyno numbers.",
      
