          "popupAnimationName": "",
      
          "drivingExperience": "The USA CLK 55 AMG delivers the same essential experience as the global car: huge naturally aspirated torque, immediate throttle response, and a compact old-school AMG coupe feel that later cars struggle to replicate. The quoted 342 hp figure does not make the car meaningfully slower in real use; it still feels muscular, urgent, and much quicker than most traffic. What stands out most is the effortlessness. The car surges forward on a thick wave of torque, and the V8 never feels strained. The transmission suits it beautifully, and the whole package feels more cohesive than many modern high-output cars. In North American ownership, the OBD-II friendliness and strong engine durability make it one of the best AMG classics to use regularly rather than just collect.",
      
          "mechanicVerdict": "The USA M113.981 remains one of the smartest classic AMG engines to buy if condition and history are right. The power rating difference is not the story; the story is that this is still the same deeply robust 5.4L naturally aspirated AMG V8 with excellent real-world durability. The core engine is rarely the financial problem. Instead, ownership quality depends on ignition discipline, cooling-system health, transmission servicing, leak management, and the overall condition of the chassis and electronics. The risk rating of 3 reflects the age and cost of maintaining an AMG properly, not a fragile design. For a North American buyer who wants a serious AMG that can actually be driven and maintained sensibly, the CLK 55 AMG is one of the best choices from the period.",
      
          "repairCostEstimate": "1,900 - 2,700 EUR / year for a well-maintained example, with neglected cars often requiring 3,500-6,800 EUR in catch-up work. Typical spending centers on ignition service, mounts, cooling hardware, transmission service, fluid leaks, and aging AMG-specific components."
