          "timingType": "Race-derived chain-driven DOHC V12 architecture with four valves per cylinder and two camshafts per bank. The M297.980 follows the Mercedes-AMG large-displacement V12 philosophy, but in the CLK GTR the timing system is not a simple luxury-car compromise adapted for road use. It is a homologation-grade setup engineered for very high load capacity, high-RPM stability, and race-bred operating conditions. The timing chains and guides are extremely robust, but the real condition of the system depends not only on mileage, but also on heat history, storage conditions, service quality, and how often the engine has been properly exercised. On a car like this, cold-start sound, oil-pressure build-up, and top-end mechanical noise tell you more than any generic mileage rule ever could. A healthy example will have a stable, composed valvetrain sound once oil pressure stabilizes, while any persistent mechanical rattle is a serious specialist-inspection issue. The timing system itself is durable, but any rebuild is highly specialized, low-volume, and extremely expensive.",
    
          "injectionType": "Sequential multi-point port injection with bespoke AMG/Bosch motorsport-influenced engine management. The road-going CLK GTR does not use an ordinary luxury-V12 fuel strategy, but a system designed to preserve race-car response while allowing the car to function on the road. The fuel delivery is extremely sharp, precise, and immediate under load changes, which is a major part of the car's character. The engine reacts very quickly to throttle movement, and when correctly calibrated it delivers the kind of brutally clean, linear response that only a large naturally aspirated racing-derived engine can provide. At the same time, the system is sensitive to throttle synchronization, intake sealing, sensor health, and exact fueling accuracy. A CLK GTR with weak sensors, stale fuel, or a poorly set-up induction side can feel noticeably compromised even if the engine itself is mechanically healthy. Because so many parts are unique or low-volume, even relatively small injection-related issues must be treated as serious preservation items rather than ordinary maintenance tasks. A properly sorted M297.980 should feel immediate, hard-edged, and astonishingly clean in its response from idle to full load.",
    
          "engineLifespan": "This is not a conventional mileage-based road-car engine. Realistically, 80,000-150,000+ km is possible in the right conditions, but the true measure of an M297.980 is not distance traveled. The important factors are specialist maintenance, correct warm-up and storage procedures, originality, heat-cycle history, and whether the car has been exercised regularly without abuse. The internals are fundamentally very strong and heavily overbuilt, because the engine comes from a homologation and competition-oriented philosophy rather than a normal grand-touring program. In practice, the greatest risks usually come from lubrication-system condition, cooling efficiency, old seals, aging electronics, fuel-system degradation, and inactivity-related deterioration rather than from the crankshaft, block, or rotating assembly itself. A properly preserved CLK GTR engine can remain extremely strong for a very long time, but ownership belongs to the world of collector motorsport rather than everyday supercar use. The engine rewards expert care and punishes neglect, poor storage, and non-specialist intervention very quickly. Its lifespan is therefore best understood as condition-dependent rather than distance-dependent.",
    
          "turboType": "Naturally aspirated. One of the defining traits of the M297.980 is that it makes all of its performance without turbochargers or supercharging. There is no boost delay, no intercooler system, no forced-induction plumbing, and no artificial torque spike. Instead, the 6.9-liter V12 produces its power through displacement, airflow, high-efficiency cylinder heads, aggressive but sophisticated calibration, and racing-derived breathing. That gives the CLK GTR its violent but perfectly linear throttle response and a power curve that feels more like a road-legal endurance racer than a traditional road supercar. The naturally aspirated layout also eliminates many of the failure points associated with forced induction, but it does not make the engine simple or cheap to maintain. It remains a highly stressed, very exotic, high-thermal-load engine package. The simplicity here is philosophical rather than economical: fewer boost systems, but much more pure mechanical performance.",
    
          "ecuType": "Bespoke AMG/Bosch motorsport-style ECU with road-homologation calibration. The purpose of the control system is not luxury refinement, but to make a race-bred V12 start, idle, and drive acceptably on the road without losing its essential competition-derived character. The ECU manages ignition, fueling, throttle response, idle control, and protective strategies in a way that allows the engine to remain tractable at low speed while still delivering immediate full-load aggression. Diagnosis is far more specialized than on a normal Mercedes, and the originality of the control system is an important part of the car's collector value. Any non-factory calibration or undocumented electronic modification is a significant risk to both drivability and long-term value. A healthy system makes the engine feel crisp, mechanical, and shockingly responsive for such a large-capacity V12. In practical terms, the ECU is part of the car's identity and should be treated accordingly.",
    
          "euroStandard": "Limited-series road-homologation compliance",
          "maxPowerStockInternals": "Around 630-650 hp may be theoretically possible with very mild intake, exhaust, and calibration refinement, but on a CLK GTR that is largely irrelevant. The real priority is preserving factory originality, thermal stability, and documented authenticity rather than chasing a small power increase. The engine almost certainly has internal headroom, but the value of the car is tied far more to correctness than to modified performance. In this context, proper factory output is more desirable than a higher dyno number. On a car of this rarity, originality is worth more than extra horsepower.",
    
