          "yearRange": "1999",
    
          "timingType": "The SuperSport retains the same race-derived chain-driven DOHC V12 timing philosophy as the standard CLK GTR, but the enlarged 7.3-liter displacement raises the mechanical stakes further. The timing system remains fundamentally strong and appropriate for very high load operation, yet the larger-capacity engine places even greater emphasis on perfect lubrication, stable operating temperature, and correct specialist setup. This is still not an engine that should be evaluated by generic mileage thinking. The condition of the timing system depends as much on storage, proper warm-up, and professional servicing as on actual distance covered. In a well-preserved SuperSport, the valvetrain should sound authoritative but controlled, with no sustained abnormal chain or guide noise after oil pressure stabilizes. Any persistent mechanical noise is a major red flag because access, parts, and the consequences of incorrect work are all exceptionally serious. The system is durable, but only in the context of expert stewardship.",
    
          "injectionType": "Sequential multi-point port injection with bespoke AMG/Bosch race-derived management, calibrated specifically for the larger 7.3-liter SuperSport engine. The enlarged displacement gives the SuperSport even more immediate torque and a heavier sense of thrust at any throttle opening. The injection system remains exceptionally precise, but because the engine moves even more air and fuel than the already extreme 6.9-liter version, calibration accuracy becomes even more critical. The character is intensely direct, with near-instant load response and an unusually raw connection between throttle input and vehicle acceleration. Correct fueling, exact throttle synchronization, strong fuel pressure, and perfect intake integrity are essential. This is not a forgiving setup where small vacuum leaks or sensor drift go unnoticed. On a SuperSport, those issues can blunt one of the most special naturally aspirated engine responses ever fitted to a road car. When healthy, the system feels savage, clean, and unbelievably immediate.",
    
          "engineLifespan": "Condition-dependent, specialist-dependent, and preservation-dependent rather than traditionally mileage-dependent. The 7.3-liter SuperSport engine is even more of a collector-grade mechanical object than the standard CLK GTR unit. The enlarged displacement increases thermal load and value sensitivity at the same time, meaning the true concern is not how many kilometers it can theoretically survive, but whether it has been stored, started, exercised, serviced, and documented correctly. Internally, it remains an immensely strong AMG V12 with very substantial reserves, but the risks around it are magnified by rarity and cost. Cooling system condition, fuel system integrity, seal health, oil-system discipline, and component originality matter more than abstract mileage claims. A properly preserved SuperSport engine can remain very healthy for a long time, but it belongs entirely to the realm of specialist collector stewardship. It is not a casual-use hypercar engine. It is a historically important, low-volume, race-related AMG V12 that requires corresponding treatment.",
    
          "turboType": "Naturally aspirated. The SuperSport version remains free of turbochargers and superchargers, and that is central to its identity. Its extraordinary output comes from a much larger 7.3-liter capacity, aggressive breathing, and high-end AMG engineering rather than any forced-induction assistance. The result is one of the most extreme naturally aspirated road-car torque curves ever associated with a Mercedes-derived engine. There is no lag, no delayed hit, and no artificial swell of boost. Instead, the engine delivers an overwhelming wall of immediate torque and a race-bred top-end that feels both massive and sharply responsive. The naturally aspirated layout preserves purity, sound, and throttle immediacy, but it does not reduce the seriousness of the package. This is still a highly exotic, high-thermal-load, low-volume V12 with enormous preservation demands. What it gains is purity of response, not ease of ownership.",
    
          "ecuType": "Bespoke AMG/Bosch motorsport-style ECU with SuperSport-specific road calibration. The larger 7.3-liter engine requires its own fueling, ignition, and throttle behavior logic, and the ECU is a major part of what makes the SuperSport feel so intense without becoming undriveable at low speed. It manages a huge naturally aspirated V12 that produces extreme torque almost everywhere, while still allowing the car to operate as a road-legal homologation special. As with the standard GTR, the originality of the control system is part of the vehicle's value. Any undocumented software or electronic modification is a serious collector and technical concern. Diagnosis is highly specialist work, not generic workshop scanning. A healthy SuperSport ECU setup makes the engine feel monumental, immediate, and shockingly clean in response given its size. It is one of the invisible reasons the car feels so complete and so terrifyingly capable.",
    
          "euroStandard": "Limited-series road-homologation compliance",
          "maxPowerStockInternals": "The SuperSport is already the peak road-going expression of this engine family in the CLK GTR context. Very small theoretical gains may exist through calibration and exhaust detail work, but in practice any tuning is almost entirely unjustified. The correct objective is preservation of the original 7.3-liter specification, documented condition, and factory behavior. The collector value of the car far outweighs the relevance of extra power. In real terms, the engine is already beyond the point where modification improves the ownership proposition.",
    
