            "timingType": "Duplex roller timing chain driving dual overhead camshafts, with a separate dedicated chain for the dual balance shafts. The fleet-variant M271.940 is mechanically identical to the standard 163 hp version in terms of block, head, crankshaft, and timing hardware. The reduced 143 hp output is achieved through software calibration, possibly a smaller throttle opening strategy, and emissions-focused tuning rather than any mechanical weakening of the engine. This means the balance shaft sprocket and timing chain vulnerabilities are every bit as real on the fleet car as on the full-power variant. In fact, fleet vehicles often suffer from deferred maintenance because they are treated as disposable company assets, which makes the predictable timing system failures even more common in the used market. The balance shaft gears use the same material and tooth profile that is prone to accelerated wear, and the chain elongates in the same way. Cold-start rattle from the front cover, irregular idle, and timing-related fault codes are the exact same warning signs. Any buyer of a former fleet or tax-optimized CLK 200 Kompressor must budget immediately for the revised balance shaft sprocket and chain kit, because the car's history almost certainly includes long oil change intervals and minimal preventive investment. Once corrected, the lower-stress calibration of the fleet engine actually makes it a slightly more relaxed long-term proposition than the higher-output version, but only if the catastrophic hardware defects are addressed first.",
        
            "injectionType": "Sequential multi-port manifold injection with Bosch ME-SFI 2.0 engine management, electronic throttle, and continuously variable intake valve timing. The fleet calibration leans toward fuel economy and emissions compliance, which alters the part-throttle behavior but does not change the fundamental injection hardware. The same intake manifold with dual tumble flaps is present, and the same pivot-bearing failure risk applies. The lower power output means the engine is less frequently driven at full load, which in theory reduces the mechanical stress on the swirl flaps, but age and heat cycles are the real enemy regardless of driving style. The supercharger bypass strategy may be slightly different to optimize low-load efficiency, but the Eaton M65 unit, its clutch, and the intercooler circuit are identical. Fleet cars are notorious for receiving the cheapest available fuel and extended oil drain intervals, which accelerates HFM contamination, injector deposits, and crankcase breather clogging. A well-sorted former fleet car should feel smooth and economical, but many suffer from accumulated neglect that masks the underlying decent design. The HFM sensor, throttle body, and supercharger clutch all require the same disciplined care as on any other M271.",
        
            "engineLifespan": "180,000-300,000+ km possible, but fleet history dramatically affects the real outcome. The fleet variant is mechanically identical to the standard car, so the same hard limits apply: balance shaft sprocket wear, timing chain stretch, and tumble flap bearing failure can all destroy the engine well before 150,000 km if ignored. However, because the 143 hp tune is gentler on the pistons, bearings, and supercharger, a properly corrected and maintained fleet engine can be a surprisingly durable daily driver. The lower thermal and mechanical stress means the pistons, rings, and big-end bearings experience less peak load. The danger lies in the typical fleet maintenance culture: minimal servicing, long oil drain intervals, ignored warning lights, and deferred everything. A used fleet CLK 200 Kompressor often arrives on the second-hand market with multiple latent problems that could have been prevented. If the balance shaft timing is corrected, the intake manifold is made safe, the supercharger is serviced, and the oil cooler leaks are fixed, this lower-output engine has the same fundamental longevity as its more powerful sibling. In some respects, it is actually a more rational choice for a budget-conscious owner who wants the C209 chassis without the insurance and fuel costs of the larger engines, provided the catch-up maintenance is budgeted honestly.",
        
            "turboType": "Supercharged. The fleet M271.940 retains the identical Eaton M65 Roots-type supercharger as the 163 hp variant. The 143 hp output is not achieved by removing boost or changing the supercharger hardware; it is a calibration and possibly throttle-limiting strategy that keeps the engine in a more economical operating window. The supercharger therefore spins, pressurizes, and generates heat in exactly the same way, which means all supercharger-related maintenance items remain relevant. The clutch pulley, drive belt, internal oil, and intercooler pump still age and fail on the same schedule. From a driving perspective, the fleet car feels less urgent and the boost threshold is managed more conservatively, but the mechanical whine and instant low-RPM torque are still present. The fleet tune may actually be easier on the supercharger long-term because the clutch engages less aggressively and the peak thermal load is lower. For a daily commute or relaxed highway use, the 143 hp Kompressor is perfectly adequate, though it lacks the sharper edge of the full-output version. The supercharger remains the defining feature of the engine character, and its health is still central to the ownership experience.",
        
            "ecuType": "Bosch ME-SFI 2.0 with fleet-specific fuel, ignition, and supercharger management calibration. The ECU is programmed for lower power, better cold-start emissions, and improved part-throttle economy. It still manages the variable intake timing, electronic throttle, knock control, and diagnostics with the same hardware as the standard car. Because fleet vehicles often accumulate fault codes that are ignored or cleared superficially, a used example may arrive with a history of unresolved mixture, misfire, or emissions codes that point to neglected sensors or air leaks rather than true ECU failure. The control unit itself is durable and not a common failure point. The fleet calibration is generally smoother and less aggressive than the 163 hp map, which some owners prefer for relaxed driving. Mild remapping to restore or slightly exceed standard output is possible, but only after all mechanical preventive work is complete. The ECU is only as reliable as the sensors and actuators it depends on, so a thorough inspection of the HFM, camshaft adjusters, and throttle body is essential before any software changes are considered.",
        
            "euroStandard": "Euro 4",
            "maxPowerStockInternals": "200-215 hp is theoretically achievable by restoring standard or mild over-standard calibration, combined with intake and exhaust improvements, because the hardware is fundamentally the same as the 163 hp engine. A simple ECU remap alone can often recover the missing 20 hp and add useful torque, bringing the fleet car close to or beyond the original higher-output specification. However, this only makes sense after the balance shafts, timing chain, and intake manifold are secured. Without that foundation, adding power accelerates the already stressed mechanical components. The safe and practical limit for a fleet car that has received the full preventive overhaul is roughly equivalent to the standard 163-184 hp range. Beyond that, the same thermal and mechanical ceilings apply as to any M271. The fleet variant is therefore best viewed as a slightly underrated engine waiting to be restored to health rather than a permanently weaker powerplant.",
        
