                "commonIssues": [
                  "Ignition Coil Destruction from Neglected Spark Plugs: The dual-spark system requires all 12 plugs to be replaced on schedule. Neglected plugs force the 6 dual-output coils to overwork, causing premature and expensive coil failure.",
                  "Valve Cover Gasket Leaks: The aluminum valve covers weep oil with age, particularly into spark plug tubes, causing misfires and coil contamination.",
                  "Oil Cooler Leaks: The oil cooler near the filter housing leaks at gaskets and seals, causing oil loss into coolant or externally.",
                  "Intake Manifold Gasket Oil Leak: Oil leaks past the intake manifold gasket, sometimes entering the coolant or creating external seepage.",
                  "HFM (Hot Film Mass) Sensor Failure: Contamination causes hesitation, poor acceleration, rich running, and fault codes.",
                  "Engine Mount Collapse: Hydraulic mounts soften and leak, transmitting vibration and harshness into the cabin.",
                  "Cooling System Aging: Expansion tanks, water pumps, thermostats, hoses, and radiators all age out. The aluminum V6 does not tolerate overheating.",
                  "Crankshaft Position Sensor Failure: A common Mercedes issue of this era causing stalling, no-start, or erratic running.",
                  "Camshaft Position Sensor Failure: Can cause rough running, hard starting, and fault codes.",
                  "Exhaust Flex Pipe and Catalytic Converter Aging: Front flex pipe cracks; catalytic converter can clog after very high mileage.",
                  "722.6 Automatic Connector and Conductor Plate Issues: The familiar 722.6 electrical and fluid-migration problems apply.",
                  "Throttle Body Carbon Buildup and Sensor Wear: The electronic throttle body can become dirty or develop position-sensor irregularities.",
                  "Oil Leaks from Front and Rear Crank Seals: Age-related seal leakage becomes significant if ignored.",
                  "Idle Air Control Valve Aging: Can cause unstable idle and stalling on early-production examples."
                ],
          
                "vulnerabilities": [
                  "Dual-Spark Maintenance Cost: The 12-plug, 6-coil ignition system is the single most expensive routine maintenance item. This is mandatory, not optional, and defines the ownership budget.",
                  "Oil Leak Multiplicity: Multiple leak points can develop simultaneously: valve covers, oil cooler, intake manifold, front and rear seals.",
                  "HFM Sensor as a Drivability Critical Point: A failed HFM sensor creates hesitation, mixture faults, and poor economy that are easy to misdiagnose as transmission or ECU problems.",
                  "Cooling System Sensitivity: The all-aluminum construction is less forgiving of overheating than iron-block engines.",
                  "Age-Stacked Ownership Costs: Like all 20-plus-year-old Mercedes, multiple medium-cost jobs accumulate together: mounts, seals, hoses, sensors, and cooling components.",
                  "Market Value vs. Repair Economics: A neglected CLK 320 can require more in ignition, gasket, and cooling catch-up work than its market value supports.",
                  "Cheap Aftermarket Ignition Parts: Low-grade coils and plugs on a dual-spark engine create more problems than they solve and can destroy the ignition system rapidly.",
                  "Transmission Neglect: The 722.6 is strong enough for the 310 Nm but requires disciplined maintenance to avoid conductor plate issues.",
                  "Rust and Chassis Deterioration: Even with an excellent engine, a rusty C209 is not a rational restoration candidate."
                ],
          
