                "commonIssues": [
                  "Rear Main Seal Leakage: The M113's most famous external weakness. The crankshaft rear main seal hardens with age and leaks oil at the transmission bellhousing junction. Not catastrophic but messy and annoying.",
                  "Ignition Coil Destruction from Neglected Spark Plugs: The dual-spark system requires all 16 plugs to be replaced on schedule. Neglected plugs force the 8 dual-output coils to overwork, causing premature and expensive coil failure.",
                  "Valve Cover Gasket Leaks: The aluminum valve covers weep oil with age, particularly into spark plug tubes, causing misfires and coil contamination on the outer bank.",
                  "Engine Mount Collapse: The heavy V8 quickly destroys hydraulic mounts, transmitting severe vibration and harshness into the cabin.",
                  "HFM (Hot Film Mass) Sensor Failure: Contamination causes hesitation, poor acceleration, rich running, and fault codes.",
                  "Oil Cooler Leaks: The oil-to-coolant heat exchanger near the filter housing leaks at gaskets and seals, causing oil loss into coolant or externally.",
                  "Intake Manifold Gasket Oil Leak: Oil leaks past the intake manifold gasket, sometimes entering the coolant or creating external seepage.",
                  "Cooling System Aging: Expansion tanks, water pumps, thermostats, hoses, and radiators all age out. The large V8 generates significant heat and does not tolerate overheating.",
                  "Crankshaft Position Sensor Failure: A common Mercedes issue of this era causing stalling, no-start, or erratic running.",
                  "Camshaft Position Sensor Failure: Can cause rough running, hard starting, and fault codes.",
                  "Exhaust Flex Pipe and Catalytic Converter Aging: The large V8's heat output fatigues the front flex pipe; catalytic converters can clog after very high mileage.",
                  "722.6 Automatic Connector and Conductor Plate Issues: The familiar 722.6 electrical and fluid-migration problems apply, though the transmission is well-matched to the V8's torque.",
                  "Throttle Body Carbon Buildup and Sensor Wear: The electronic throttle body can become dirty or develop position-sensor irregularities.",
                  "Oil Leaks from Front Crank Seal: Age-related seal leakage becomes significant with the high-mileage examples.",
                  "Idle Air Control Valve Aging: Can cause unstable idle on early-production examples.",
                  "Crankcase Breather / Oil Separator Clogging: Increased blow-by forces oil past seals and increases consumption.",
                  "Alternator and Belt Tensioner Wear: The V8's accessory drive loads accelerate alternator bearing and tensioner wear."
                ],
          
                "vulnerabilities": [
                  "Dual-Spark Maintenance Cost: The 16-plug, 8-coil ignition system is the single most expensive routine maintenance item. Mandatory, predictable, and defines the ownership budget.",
                  "Rear Main Seal Inevitability: Nearly every high-mileage M113 will eventually seep from the rear main seal. Not fatal, but a persistent nuisance.",
                  "HFM Sensor as a Drivability Critical Point: A failed MAF creates hesitation and mixture faults that are easy to misdiagnose as transmission or ECU problems.",
                  "Heavy Engine, Weak Mounts: The V8's mass destroys mounts faster than smaller engines. Collapsed mounts accelerate driveline wear and cabin harshness.",
                  "Cooling System Sensitivity: The large-displacement V8 generates serious heat. Overheating is immediately damaging to the Alusil block.",
                  "Age-Stacked Ownership Costs: A 20-plus-year-old performance Mercedes accumulates multiple medium-cost jobs: mounts, seals, hoses, sensors, and cooling components.",
                  "Fuel Economy Penalty: The 5.0L V8 consumes significantly more fuel than six-cylinder alternatives, making it expensive to run daily.",
                  "Market Value vs. Repair Economics: A neglected CLK 500 can require more in ignition, gasket, and cooling catch-up work than its market value supports.",
                  "Cheap Aftermarket Ignition Parts: Low-grade coils and plugs on a dual-spark engine create more problems than they solve and can destroy the ignition system rapidly.",
                  "Transmission Heat: The 722.6 handles the 460 Nm well but generates significant heat; fluid and cooler condition must be monitored."
                ],
          
