                    "author": ".",
                
                    "drivingExperience": "The 224 hp CLK 320 CDI is arguably the best all-round diesel powertrain ever fitted to the C209 platform. It combines genuine performance with grand-tourer refinement and exceptional real-world economy. The 510 Nm of torque, when paired with the 7G-Tronic automatic, delivers effortlessly rapid acceleration, quiet high-speed cruising, and remarkable flexibility for overtaking. The V6 is significantly smoother and more cultured than the inline-four or inline-five diesels, making long journeys genuinely relaxing. The engine does not feel like a compromise; it feels like a performance choice that happens to use diesel fuel. In the manual transmission variant, the torque limit to 415 Nm is noticeable but still leaves the car feeling strong and flexible. The CLK 320 CDI's character is that of a rapid, refined express coupe—fast enough to entertain, sensible enough to use every day, and comfortable enough to cross continents.",
                
                    "mechanicVerdict": "From a mechanic's perspective, the OM642.910 is a magnificent engine when healthy and a potentially expensive one when neglected. It demands more attentive ownership than the simpler OM612 or OM646: the oil cooler seals, swirl flaps, DPF, EGR, and complex V-bank packaging all add cost and labor to maintenance. However, none of these issues are design catastrophes—they are simply the price of a modern, high-output, emissions-compliant V6 diesel. If the oil cooler seals are addressed preventively, the intake swirl system is kept functional or deleted intelligently, and the transmission is serviced on schedule, this engine rewards its owner with exceptional performance and durability. If these items are ignored, the repair bills escalate quickly. The 7G-Tronic automatic is the preferred pairing; the manual's torque limit and DMF fragility make it less suited to the engine's character. For a buyer willing to maintain it properly, the CLK 320 CDI is the diesel CLK to have.",
                
                    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000-2,000 EUR per year is realistic for a well-maintained example. Oil cooler seal replacement runs 700-1,500 EUR, swirl system repairs 250-800 EUR, EGR and DPF cleaning or repair 300-800 EUR, turbo actuator replacement 300-600 EUR, and 7G-Tronic service 300-450 EUR. A neglected example requiring multiple simultaneous repairs can easily demand 2,000-4,000 EUR in its first year of proper restoration."
